#
# `kill` and a job specification — #2318's dash/ half.
#
# dash takes `%1` in `wait`, which core/jobs.tests asks and every column
# answers alike. It does not take one in `kill`: a job spec there is a word
# `kill` cannot make a pid out of, and the command fails. bash, zsh and ksh93
# all accept it.
#
# That matters because `kill %1` is how a script that started something in the
# background stops it again, usually from a trap on the way out. Here it has
# to be the pid, which the last case keeps and waits on — so this is a missing
# spelling rather than a missing capability, and a portable script simply
# keeps the pid. `kill "$pid"` itself is not asked, because a signal sent to a
# job this file has not waited for is delivery order, and delivery
# order does not repeat.
#
# ---- the job has to still be running ----
#
# Both `kill` cases were asked about `( exit 0 ) &` — a child that is already
# finished by the time the next line runs, usually. Whether `%1` still names
# something to signal is then a question about how far the shell has got with
# reaping it, and that is a race rather than an answer: zsh reported both ways
# for `kill -0 %1`, which made the file non-deterministic and cost the dash
# column its whole run, since a suite that ships no expected output has
# nothing to grade a file the reference will not repeat against (#2963).
#
# So each `kill` is asked about a job that cannot have finished. The job
# blocks opening a fifo for reading, which cannot complete until this script
# opens the other end, so the child is alive for certain when `kill` is asked
# about it and exits normally — status 0, no signal, nothing for the shell to
# report asynchronously — once the case is done with it. No `sleep`, because
# a job that is alive because it has not got round to exiting yet is the race
# again with a longer fuse.
#
# The discriminating claim survives that change, because it never depended on
# the child being finished: dash alone cannot make a pid out of the *word*.
#
# What did not survive is the second line's boundary, and holding the job open
# is what showed it up. Asked about a finished job, ksh93 refused
# `kill -s CONT %1` ("no such job") and bash and zsh took it — so the line was
# written down as a spelling ksh93 joins dash in refusing. Asked about a
# running one, ksh93 takes it like the other two, and refuses it only once
# the job is gone. Measured 2026-09-15 on ksh93 (AT&T 93u 2012-08-01), both
# ways. That was never a fact about `kill -s CONT %1`; it was the same race
# the first line was caught by, read as a dialect difference. Both lines now
# say the one thing: dash is alone.
#
# Nothing here prints a pid or a job number; each `kill` is asked as whether
# it succeeded, and every job is waited for before the next one starts, so no
# case depends on which process the system ran first.

mkfifo gate
cat < gate > /dev/null &
kill -0 %1 2> /dev/null
printf 'kill -0 on a job spec -> %s\n' "$(( $? == 0 ))"
: > gate
wait
rm -f gate

mkfifo gate
cat < gate > /dev/null &
kill -s CONT %1 2> /dev/null
printf 'kill -s CONT on a job spec -> %s\n' "$(( $? == 0 ))"
: > gate
wait
rm -f gate

( exit 0 ) &
p=$!
wait "$p"
printf 'wait on the pid instead -> %s\n' "$?"
printf 'done\n'
